  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 AM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge that was in place over Wyoming on Saturday has \nbegun to fold over and flatten as of 1AM. Water vapor imagery is \nalready showing more zonal flow across northwest Wyoming. By mid to \nlate morning, zonal flow will prevail across the state with a \nsurface high centered over Colorado. Although 700mb temperatures \nwill be in the 4C to 8C degree range, deep mixing is not expected \nfor most locations and these temperatures will likely not translate \nto the surface. Nonetheless, another warm and dry day is expected \nwith highs in the 50s west of the Continental Divide and upper 50s \nand 60s for most locations east of the Continental Divide. \n\nZonal flow aloft is forecast to strengthen throughout the day over \nnorthern Wyoming. This is particularly favorable for gusty winds \nacross Yellowstone National Park and the Absarokas, around Dubois, \nand for downsloping to occur off of the Bighorns into Johnson \nCounty. Temperatures across Johnson County will have no issue \nwarming into the low 70s today with many locations seeing minimum \nrelative humidity values dropping into the 15% to 20% range. Gusty \nwinds are also forecast from South Pass to southeast Johnson County \nand eastern Natrona County ahead of a weak cold front this evening. \nGiven the abnormally warm temperatures, low relative humidity \nvalues, and breezy winds across Johnson County, elevated to near \ncritical fire weather conditions are expected this afternoon. \n\nZonal flow will remain present across the state on Monday with \nbreezy conditions across many of the same locations as today due to \nfairly strong flow aloft. Most will see similar temperatures to \ntoday and more dry weather on Monday. Johnson County will be the \nexception due to forecasted 700mb temperatures being decently cooler \n(1C vs the 8C today) behind this evening's cold front. There is \nconsensus amongst model guidance that a shortwave trough will \ntraverse the area on Tuesday. There is forecast to be just enough \nmoisture and orographic lift for light showers (20% chance) across \nnorthwest Wyoming. Most will remain dry with warm temperatures and \nbreezy afternoon winds.\n\nConfidence continues to increase that an upper level ridge will \ndominate the region Wednesday through Saturday. Current 700mb and \n500mb temperatures accompanying this ridge are above the 90th \npercentile of climatology. This means that dry weather and \nabnormally warm temperatures will linger as we enter the middle of \nNovember. In fact, the current forecasted highs in the 50s and 60s \nare 10 to 20 degrees above normal. So if you are ready for the \ncooler November temperatures, you will have to wait just a bit\nlonger.",
